The code statements that lead to the bug are:
p = names;
for (i = 0; i < ue->info.value.enumerated.items; ++i) {
    name_len = strnlen(p, buf_len);
    ...
    p += name_len + 1;
    buf_len -= name_len + 1;
}
Reason:
The loop decrements `buf_len` each iteration by `name_len + 1`. If the total length of the names is exactly exhausted by the time the last name is processed, `buf_len` becomes zero before the next iteration. The next call to `strnlen(p, 0)` with `buf_len == 0` invokes the fortified `strnlen()` macro, which validates `maxlen` against the object size of `p`. Due to pointer arithmetic, the compiler may lose track of the object size, causing a BRK exception panic before the return value is checked.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
for (i = 0; i < ue->info.value.enumerated.items; ++i) {
+	if (buf_len == 0) {
+		kvfree(names);
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
	name_len = strnlen(p, buf_len);
    ...
}
Reason:
Adding the `if (buf_len == 0)` check at the beginning of each loop iteration ensures that `strnlen()` is never called with a zero `buf_len` when items still remain. This prevents the fortified `strnlen()` from triggering a panic due to an invalid maxlen argument, and returns `-EINVAL` as a proper error condition.

